        "paragraph_id": "6B21FEF7",
        "text": "Radiation stresses"
      },
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_master.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 15922,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 424,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 374,
        "paragraph_id": "123F2CF0",
        "text": "Given the spatial distribution of the wave action (and therefore wave energy) the radiation stresses can be evaluated by using linear wave theory as described by:"
      }
    ],
    "following_nonempty_paragraphs": [
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_master.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 16038,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 427,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 377,
        "paragraph_id": "4ACC26CC",
        "text": "Wave shape"
      },
      {
        "container_path": "models/XBeach/raw/source_code/trunk/doc/manual/XBeach_manual_master.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 16060,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 428,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 378,
        "paragraph_id": "14F94006",
        "text": "The morphodynamic model considered is (short) wave averaged and resolves hydrodynamics associated with the wave group time scale. As a result the short wave shape is not solved for. However, as waves propagate from deep water onto beaches, their surface form and orbital water motion become increasingly non-linear because of the amplification of the higher harmonics."
      }
    ],
